  client-set because [38;5;153mkeepalive[39m requests can arrive late, making receipt time unreliable. I deliberately left out an
  idempotency key — with fire-and-forget and no retry there are no duplicates to guard against.

  [1mTwo calls, not one.[0m [38;5;153mlogin_succeeded[39m carries the [38;5;153muserId[39m; [38;5;153mlogin_failed[39m carries [38;5;153muserId: null[39m plus the attempted username.
  The failed case is why identity is nullable throughout.

  [1mTwo judgment calls I made rather than asked about[0m — push back on either:

  1. [1mThe tracking calls go inside [38;5;153mlogin[39m, not in the submit handler.[0m Your requirement is "every login is recorded," and
     putting it in the handler makes that depend on each caller remembering. The cost is that [38;5;153mlogin[39m is no longer purely
     authentication.
  2. [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m stays synchronous.[0m It's still a stub with a [38;5;153mTODO[39m for the real API POST. Making it async is an auth-flow
     change, and tracking doesn't need it — [38;5;153mtrack[39m is never awaited. Worth doing when the real endpoint lands, but not
     this change.

  [1mOne assumption you should check.[0m [38;5;153mlogin[39m currently returns [38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m with no user id anywhere in the client. For
  the flow to work, the stub's return has to gain a [38;5;153muserId[39m field standing in for what the real auth response would
  carry. That's invented data until the backend exists — the tracking pipeline will be real and testable, but what it
  reports is a placeholder until both endpoints are built.
